// std.io (CLI-completion slice 1): the program's two observable output streams. `io.out`/`io.outln`
// write to the SAME stdout buffer the `echo` keyword uses (so they interleave in program order);
// `io.err`/`io.errln` write to stderr. Both streams are observable output routed through the
// backends' buffers, so the differential oracle pins BOTH — the tree-walker and the VM (and, under
// `--features jit`, tier 0 vs tier 1) must produce byte-identical stdout AND stderr here. The split
// between the two streams is asserted directly via `// expect: stdout` / `// expect: stderr`.
// expect: stdout "e1"
// expect: stdout "out"
// expect: stdout "42"
// expect: stdout "e2"
// expect: stderr "warn"
// expect: stderr "second"
// expect: exit 0
use std.io

// An `echo` line and `io.out` fragments share one stdout buffer, in order.
echo "e1"
io.out("o")
io.out("ut")
io.outln("")

// `io.outln` renders any value the way `echo` does — here an int, no quotes.
io.outln(42)

// stderr is its own buffer: these do not appear on stdout.
io.errln("warn")
io.err("sec")
io.errln("ond")

echo "e2"
